[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Account Abstraction (ERC-4337): Paymasters, Bundlers, and UserOps"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Account Abstraction, specifically through the ERC-4337 standard, introduces a revolutionary way to handle transactions on Ethereum and EVM-compatible chains. By separating transaction validation and execution from Externally Owned Accounts (EOAs), developers can build rich user experiences like gas sponsorship, social recovery, and batch transactions without altering the core consensus protocol."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts of ERC-4337"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"UserOperation (UserOp): A pseudo-transaction object that users create to describe the action they want to perform. Bundlers collect these into a single transaction."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Bundler: A node or actor that packages multiple UserOperations into a single on-chain transaction directed at the EntryPoint contract."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Paymaster: A smart contract that sponsors gas fees on behalf of users, enabling gasless transactions or fee payments in ERC-20 tokens."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"EntryPoint: A singleton smart contract that handles the validation and execution of UserOperations in a secure, standardized loop."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Constructing a UserOperation"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike legacy transactions, UserOps contain specific fields tailored for smart contract wallets. Below is an example structure of a UserOperation in JavaScript using viem or ethers equivalents:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"const userOp = {
  sender: '0x1234...5678', // The smart account address
  nonce: '0x0',
  initCode: '0x', // Used for deploying the wallet if it does not exist yet
  callData: '0xabcdef...', // The encoded function call to execute
  callGasLimit: '0x50000',
  verificationGasLimit: '0x150000',
  preVerificationGas: '0x21000',
  maxFeePerGas: '0x77359400',
  maxPriorityFeePerGas: '0x3b9aca00',
  paymasterAndData: '0x...', // Address and parameters for gas sponsorship
  signature: '0x...'
};"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Paymaster for Sponsored Transactions"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Paymasters allow decentralized applications to pay for user gas. To use a Paymaster, the paymasterAndData field must be populated with the Paymaster address and any required signature data proving authorization. Here is a Solidity interface example of how a basic Verifying Paymaster validates requests:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.19;

import "@account-abstraction/contracts/interfaces/IPaymaster.sol";

contract SimplePaymaster is IPaymaster {
    address public immutable verifyingSigner;

    constructor(address _signer) {
        verifyingSigner = _signer;
    }

    function validatePaymasterUserOp(
        UserOperation calldata userOp,
        bytes32 userOpHash,
        uint256 maxCost
    ) external returns (bytes memory context, uint256 validationData) {
        // Perform validation logic (e.g., check signature from verifyingSigner)
        return ("", 0);
    }

    function postOp(PostOpMode mode, bytes calldata context, uint256 actualGasCost) external {
        // Handle post-execution settlement
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Working with Bundlers"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Bundlers listen to the alternative mempool for UserOperations. Once enough UserOps are gathered, the bundler calls the handleOps function on the EntryPoint contract. Developers can interact with bundlers using JSON-RPC methods like eth_sendUserOperation."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// Example JSON-RPC request to a Bundler
{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 1,
  "method": "eth_sendUserOperation",
  "params": [
    {
      "sender": "0x1234...5678",
      "nonce": "0x0",
      "callData": "0x..."
    },
    "0x5FF137D4b0FDCD49DcA30c7CF57E578a026d2789" // EntryPoint address
  ]
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"ERC-4337 transforms how users interact with blockchain applications. By leveraging UserOps, Bundlers, and Paymasters, developers can abstract away complexities like native gas tokens and seed phrase management, driving mainstream adoption of Web3 applications."}]
